; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Why We Have Seasons
; CATEGORY: Science & Simulation
; LEVEL:    Intermediate
; TAGS:     astronomy, seasons, earth, sun, animation, trigonometry
; SUMMARY:  The tilted Earth travels around the Sun while the program shows the month and how long the day lasts at 45 degrees north.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   We look at the Earth's orbit from a little above, so the circle
;   looks squashed into an oval. The Earth's axis is tilted and always
;   points the same way in space (toward the North Star). In June the
;   north pole leans toward the Sun, so the north gets long days and
;   summer. In December it leans away and the north gets winter. The
;   dark half of the Earth is its night side. The month and the length
;   of the day at 45 degrees north, about where Minneapolis, Bordeaux and
;   Milan are, are shown at the top.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run and watch the Earth go around a few times.
;   Press Stop to finish. Change LATITUDE to see your own day lengths.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * seasons come from the tilt, not from the distance to the Sun
;   * SIN and COS to place something on an oval path
;   * ARC with a thick pen to paint half a circle (the night side)
;   * a real astronomy formula for the length of a day
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Set TILT to 0. Do the day lengths still change?
;   * Set LATITUDE to 0 (the equator) or 66 (the Arctic Circle).
;   * Set TILT to 90 for a planet like Uranus. What would summer be like?
; ============================================================

make "tilt 23.44
make "latitude 45
make "months [March April May June July August September October November December January February]

; Where is the Earth when it is :angle degrees around the orbit?
to earth.spot :angle
  output list 300 * cos :angle 140 * sin :angle
end

; How far north (positive) or south the Sun is overhead, in degrees.
to sun.height :angle
  output :tilt * sin (:angle - 90)
end

; The sunrise equation: hours of daylight at a latitude.
to day.hours :angle
  localmake "x (minus tan :latitude) * tan sun.height :angle
  ; past the polar circles the sun never sets, or never rises
  if :x < -1 [output 24]
  if :x > 1 [output 0]
  output 2 * (arccos :x) / 15
end

to draw.sun
  setpc [255 200 0] setpensize 70 penup home dot pos
end

to draw.earth :angle
  localmake "spot earth.spot :angle
  penup setpos :spot
  setpc [40 110 220] setpensize 40 dot pos
  ; the night side: half a ring as thick as the Earth's radius, painted
  ; on the side facing away from the sun
  setheading (towards [0 0]) + 90
  setpc [10 20 60] setpensize 10 pendown arc 180 5
  setpensize 10 arc 180 15
  penup
  ; the axis, always tilted the same way
  setpc 7 setpensize 2 setheading :tilt
  back 30 pendown fd 60 penup
  setlabelheight 14 label "N
end

to draw.frame :angle
  clean
  setpc [90 90 120] setpensize 1
  ; the orbit, drawn from many short lines
  penup setpos earth.spot 0 pendown
  for [a 0 360 5] [setpos earth.spot :a]
  penup
  ; Is the Earth behind the Sun (top of the oval)? Then draw it first,
  ; so the Sun covers it.
  ifelse (sin :angle) > 0 [draw.earth :angle draw.sun] [draw.sun draw.earth :angle]
  localmake "month item 1 + int (remainder (:angle - 90 + 360) 360) / 30 :months
  setpc 7 setlabelheight 20
  penup setxy -380 260 label (sentence [Month:] :month)
  penup setxy -380 230
  label (sentence [Daylight at] :latitude [degrees north:] form day.hours :angle 4 1 [hours])
end

clearscreen hideturtle
setbackground [5 5 25]
forever [
  for [angle 90 449 1.5] [draw.frame :angle wait 2]
]
